# Vulnerabilities that BLOCK a release are listed here as exceptions.
#
# This file is deliberately NOT named `.trivyignore`. Trivy auto-loads that name
# from the working directory, which would also hide these findings from the
# SARIF report uploaded to the Security tab. The split is the point:
#
#   report  -> everything, always, in the Security tab
#   gate    -> everything except what is listed here
#
# Only one thing belongs in this file: a vulnerability in a base image we do not
# build, where no fixed image has been published yet. Trivy marks those "fixed"
# because the upstream *library* has a fix — but a Go binary compiled into
# grafana/grafana or prom/blackbox-exporter cannot be upgraded by us. Blocking
# on those converts "upstream has not released" into "we cannot deploy".
#
# Anything in our own dependency tree does NOT belong here. Fix it instead.
#
# Every entry needs an expiry. When it lapses the finding blocks again, which
# forces a recheck rather than letting an exception become permanent.

# --- prom/blackbox-exporter:v0.28.0 ---------------------------------------
# v0.28.0 (released 2025-12-06) is the latest upstream release, confirmed
# against the GitHub releases API on 2026-08-07. It is 8 months old and was
# compiled with Go 1.25.5, so its vendored libraries and stdlib are frozen at
# that point. There is no newer image to move to.
#
# Both entries below are in the prober binary. Blackbox makes outbound HTTP(S)
# probes to our own public health endpoints; it accepts no inbound traffic, has
# no public domain, and handles no user data or credentials. That is why these
# are acceptable to carry rather than urgent.
#
# TWO CVEs from one stale image is a pattern, not bad luck. If a third appears
# before v0.29.0 ships, build the exporter from pinned upstream source on a
# current Go toolchain instead of continuing to annotate this file.

# Vendored google.golang.org/grpc v1.77.0.
CVE-2026-33186 exp:2026-11-07

# Go stdlib v1.25.5 — crypto/tls certificate validation on session resumption.
# Would require an attacker positioned between the prober and our own health
# endpoints, to make a failing probe look healthy.
CVE-2025-68121 exp:2026-11-07
